**Day 21: Introduction to NoSQL and MongoDB**  

**Topic:**  
NoSQL databases, particularly MongoDB, provide a flexible, schema-less approach to data storage, ideal for unstructured or semi-structured data. MongoDB stores data in JSON-like documents, enabling scalability and high performance for modern applications.  

**Learning Objectives:**  
• Understand the fundamentals of NoSQL databases and their advantages over relational databases.  
• Learn MongoDB’s document-oriented architecture and key features.  
• Perform basic CRUD operations in MongoDB.  

**Activities / Deliverables:**  
• Install MongoDB and set up a local development environment.  
• Create a sample database and collections using MongoDB Shell.  
• Practice inserting, querying, updating, and deleting documents.  

**Important Concepts:**  
• **Document Storage:** Data is stored as BSON (Binary JSON) documents.  
• **Collections:** Groupings of documents (analogous to tables in SQL).  
• **Scalability:** Horizontal scaling via sharding.  

**SQL Syntax / Commands:**  
• `db.createCollection("collectionName")` – Creates a new collection.  
• `db.collectionName.insert({key: value})` – Inserts a document.  
• `db.collectionName.find({query})` – Retrieves documents matching a query.  

**Real-world Use Cases:**  
• Storing user-generated content (e.g., social media posts, comments).  
• Handling large-scale IoT data with varying schemas.  

**Tools / Tech:**  
• MongoDB Server  
• MongoDB Compass (GUI tool)  
• MongoDB Shell (CLI)  

**Summary:**  
Explored NoSQL principles and MongoDB’s document-based model. Practiced foundational CRUD operations and understood its real-world applications for flexible data management.  

